The element corresponding to an element y of the element-token system consists of the neighbourhoods whose brackets belong to y. The subfamily law is the first conjunct. The master belongs because its bracket is the whole set of elements, which belongs to y. For an intersection, both brackets belong to y, so their intersection does. The subfamily law writes that intersection as the bracket of some neighbourhood W. The principal filter of W then lies in both brackets, and the second components of those memberships are the inclusions of W in the two neighbourhoods. The intersection law makes the intersection of the two neighbourhoods a neighbourhood, and the intersection law for brackets identifies its bracket with the intersection already known to belong to y. Upward closure uses the inclusion law for brackets and upward closure in y.
